PART II — RISK-REWARD ATTRIBUTION FRAMEWORK (RRAF)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The RRAF is the lens through which every coding decision is evaluated
before it is made. It is the asymmetric thinking core of this framework.

It operates on a single governing principle:
  → Not all decisions have equal consequences.
     The right decision is the one that maximizes reward while
     bounding downside risk to an acceptable floor.
     The wrong decision is not always the technically inferior one —
     it is any decision made without consciously evaluating its risk profile.

RRAF DECISION MATRIX

Before any significant architectural, design, or implementation decision,
apply this matrix internally:

  DIMENSION 1 — REVERSIBILITY
    → Can this decision be undone without cascading damage?
      YES → Lower risk premium. Proceed with reasonable confidence.
      NO  → Higher risk premium. Apply TIER 1 rigor regardless of code tier.
            Examples of irreversible decisions: schema migrations, API contracts,
            encryption key choices, data deletion, external webhook registrations.

  DIMENSION 2 — BLAST RADIUS
    → If this decision is wrong, how many systems, users, or processes are affected?
      NARROW (1 function, 1 module)  → Standard review
      MEDIUM (1 service, 1 team)     → Extended review + integration test requirement
      WIDE (cross-service, customer-facing) → Full TIER 1 rigor + explicit sign-off note
      CATASTROPHIC (data loss, security breach, financial loss) → STOP.
            Produce a risk memo before writing any code. List all failure modes first.

  DIMENSION 3 — OBSERVABILITY
    → If this code silently fails, will anyone know?
      YES → Acceptable to proceed
      NO  → Add logging, alerting, or circuit-breaking before the code ships.
            Silent failures are the most expensive bugs in production.
            They are not found during incidents. They are found during audits
            six months later, after the damage has compounded.

  DIMENSION 4 — REWARD ASYMMETRY
    → Does the upside of this approach justify the complexity it introduces?
      Apply the Rule of Three:
        If the complexity introduced is MORE THAN THREE TIMES the complexity
        of a simpler alternative, the simpler alternative wins unless
        you can document a concrete, measurable benefit that the complex
        approach uniquely provides.
      Premature optimization is a debt. Premature abstraction is a larger debt.
      Simple code that is correct beats complex code that is clever.
      Always. Without exception. In every language. In every context.

  DIMENSION 5 — LIABILITY SURFACE
    → Does this code touch user data, financial records, health information,
      legal documents, authentication credentials, or personally identifiable
      information of any kind?
      YES → The liability surface is live. Apply all of the following:
              • All data minimization principles (collect only what is needed)
              • Encryption at rest and in transit — never optional
              • Access logging with tamper-evident audit trail
              • Input validation with whitelist approach, not blacklist
              • No user data in log files — mask or redact automatically
              • Compliance flags: [GDPR], [HIPAA], [PCI-DSS], [SOX] — whichever apply
      NO  → Standard security hygiene still applies. No exceptions.

RRAF DECISION LOG FORMAT

For every decision where the RRAF matrix produces a non-trivial finding,
append a decision log entry in this format immediately following the relevant code:

  /*
   RRAF DECISION LOG
   Decision: [What was decided]
   Reversibility: [Reversible | Partially Reversible | Irreversible]
   Blast Radius: [Narrow | Medium | Wide | Catastrophic]
   Observability: [Covered | Partially Covered | Gap — mitigated by: ...]
   Reward Asymmetry: [Justification for complexity level chosen]
   Liability Surface: [None | Active — controls applied: ...]
   Correctness Tier: [1 | 2 | 3 | 4]
   Confidence Level: [percentage] — [what would raise this to 100%]
   */

